## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical graph DP with a real-world flavor (service startup), ensuring knowledge of cycle detection, topological order, and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn/DFS)
- DAG DP for path/finish times
- Cycle detection via processed node count

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestStartup(graph, startup, t) {
  const V = graph.length;
  const indeg = new Array(V).fill(0);
  for (let u = 0; u < V; u++) for (const v of graph[u]) indeg[v]++;
  const q = [];
  const earliest = startup.slice();
  for (let i = 0; i < V; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let seen = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    seen++;
    for (const v of graph[u]) {
      earliest[v] = Math.max(earliest[v], earliest[u] + startup[v]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (seen !== V) throw new Error('cycle detected');
  return earliest[t];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to dynamic updates in graph or startup times?
- How would you extend to multiple targets efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical graph reasoning: counting shortest paths under a Dijkstra framework, handling path multiplicities without overflow, and edge-case ties.

## Key Concepts

- Dijkstra with path counting
- Tie handling on relaxation
- Complexity and edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const N = graph.length;
  const dist = Array(N).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(N).fill(0);
  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.d-b.d);
  pq.push({node:s, d:0});
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
     const {node:u, d} = pq.pop();
     if (d !== dist[u]) continue;
     for (const [v, w] of graph[u]) {
        const nd = dist[u] + w;
        if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; pq.push({node:v, d:nd}); }
        else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] += ways[u]; }
     }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you apply modulo to prevent overflow for large counts?
- How would you adapt if edges had zero weight or negative cycles are possible?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to blend Trie prefix search with balanced BSTs for dynamic scores, plus a top-k retrieval strategy that scales efficiently. It tests practical update propagation and memory trade-offs in a high-throughput autocomplete system.

## Key Concepts
- Trie for prefix lookup
- AVL/balanced BST per node for phrase-score storage
- Top-k extraction via a front-to-back heap or subtree aggregation
- Update propagation costs and memory considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.words = new AVLTree(); // key: phrase, value: score
    this.topK = new MaxHeap(k); // top-k scores for subtree
  }
}

class AutoCompleteDictionary {
  constructor(k = 10) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.k = k;
  }

  insertPhrase(phrase, score) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of phrase) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.words.insert(phrase, score);
      node.topK.insert(score);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTopK(k);
  }
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis
- insertPhrase: O(L log n) where L is phrase length, n is phrases per prefix
- updateScore: O(L log n) for BST updates plus O(L) for heap propagation
- deletePhrase: O(L log n) for BST removal and heap cleanup
- queryTopK: O(L + k log k) for traversal plus heap extraction

## Memory Trade-offs
- Trie structure: O(N × L) where N is total phrases, L is average length
- BST per node: O(N) total across all nodes
- Top-k heaps: O(N × k) worst-case, but typically O(N) with pruning

## Edge Cases
- Duplicate phrases: update existing entry instead of inserting
- Score ties: use lexicographic ordering as tiebreaker
- Empty prefix: return global top-k from root node
- Large k: consider segment tree for better range queries

## Comparison with Alternatives
- Hash map + sorting: O(1) insert but O(n log n) prefix queries
- Suffix tree: better substring search but higher memory overhead
- Pure Trie with scores: O(L) queries but O(n) updates for score changes
- Hybrid approach (proposed): balances prefix efficiency with dynamic updates

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a real-time aggregated leaderboard with sliding windows and per-key deduplication. It combines hash tables, heaps, and lazy expiration techniques to achieve O(log K) top-K retrieval while maintaining constant-time lookups.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding window by timestamp**: Only events within the last W seconds contribute to the sum
- **Per-city deduplication**: Within each city, only the latest event per (city, driver_id) counts—older events from the same driver are discarded
- **City sums**: Maintained via per-city hashmaps and a max-heap for efficient ranking
- **Global top-K**: Implemented with a min-heap supporting O(log K) retrieval per update

